---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';

const videos = (await getCollection('videos')).sort((a, b) =>
  a.data.videoId.localeCompare(b.data.videoId)
);
const cataloged = videos.filter((v) => v.data.cataloged).length;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Film Footage"
  description="Larry's 8mm movie film from Vietnam, 1964-1965, digitized and awaiting his memories."
>
  <span class="eyebrow">Moving pictures</span>
  <h1>Film Footage</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Besides his slides, Larry brought home 8mm movie film — about twenty-two minutes of it,
    including scenes of Saigon. The reels were digitized at home in 2009 by projecting them on a
    wall and re-filming the projection, and that transfer is what plays below, cleaned up as far
    as software allows. Each clip has a permanent archive number and its own
    <strong>Memories</strong> box for what Larry remembers of it.
  </p>
  <p class="record">
    {videos.length} clip{videos.length === 1 ? '' : 's'} &middot; {cataloged} cataloged so far
    &middot; nothing about an uncataloged clip is guessed
  </p>

  <aside class="family-note">
    <p class="record note-heading">Note to the family — the film can look far better than this</p>
    <p>
      These web copies come from the 2009 wall transfer, and no software can put back what that
      capture lost. If the original 8mm reels still exist, a professional
      <strong>frame-by-frame scan</strong> would recover true colors, full sharpness, and steady
      frames — far beyond anything recoverable from the wall capture. It costs roughly
      <strong>$20&ndash;30 per 50-ft reel</strong> (this footage is about six reels) from mail-in
      services like <a href="https://www.scancafe.com/">ScanCafe</a> or
      <a href="https://legacybox.com/">LegacyBox</a>, or from a local camera shop. First step:
      find out whether the reels survived.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <ul class="video-grid">
    {videos.map((video) => (
      <li>
        <a href={withBase(`/footage/${video.id}/`)}>
          <span class="poster-wrap">
            <img
              src={withBase(`/videos/posters/${video.data.videoId}.jpg`)}
              alt={video.data.cataloged ? video.data.title : `Film clip ${video.data.videoId}, not yet cataloged`}
              loading="lazy"
              width="528"
              height="424"
            />
            <span class="play-mark" aria-hidden="true">&#9654;</span>
          </span>
          <span class="record video-id">{video.data.videoId}</span>
          <span class="video-title">{video.data.title}</span>
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>About this transfer</h2>
  <p>
    The digitization is itself part of the family story: LeRoy ran the reels through the family
    projector, filmed the projected image from the wall with a MiniDV camcorder, and captured the
    tape to a computer — the technology of 2009 doing its best by the technology of 1965. The raw
    captures (one 22-minute transfer and five short test takes of the same scenes) are preserved
    unaltered in the family archive on the NAS; the copies here are cropped to the projected
    image, steadied, deflickered, and brightened from that source.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }

  .family-note {
    border: 1px dashed var(--signal);
    background: var(--paper-deep);
    padding: 1.2rem 1.4rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  .note-heading {
    color: var(--signal);
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
  }

  .family-note p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .video-grid {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(14rem, 1fr));
  }

  .video-grid a {
    display: block;
    text-decoration: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--paper-deep);
    padding: 0.5rem;
  }

  .video-grid a:hover {
    border-color: var(--signal);
  }

  .poster-wrap {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
  }

  .video-grid img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
  }

  .play-mark {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-size: 2.2rem;
    color: #fff;
    text-shadow: 0 0 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.8);
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  .video-id {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.45rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .video-title {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
